e Koopa spawns only when camera is closer. Anyway, now we know the Koopa's position ~1666. \n\nLet me load slot 2 (x=1300) and run until Mario is at ~1600 and pause. Then list_sprites.\n\nActually, more reliable: run shorter increments and screenshot each time. From x=1300, run 100 frames → x~1550. Then 20 frames → ~1600. Let me do increments of 20.\n\nActually, from the current observation, the Koopa at x1666 was still there when Mario at 1697 (behind, screen 95). So the Koopa walks left. At x=1600, the Koopa would be at ~1650ish (it may have walked). Let me try: load slot2, run 110 frames → x~1575, camera~1450, Koopa at ~1640 → screen 190. Should be visible.\n\nHmm but earlier at x=1552 it wasn't visible. Let me just go stepwise: load slot 2, run 80 frames → x~1500, screenshot. If no Koopa, +20 frames, etc.\n\nActually there's a subtlety: loading slot 2 resets the Koopa state (it hasn't spawned yet at x=1300). The RNG might differ but enemy spawn is deterministic by camera position. Good.\n\nAlso: the star will kill the Koopa on contact, so I must stop before Mario's hitbox reaches it. Mario at x, Koopa at k; Mario sprite is 16 wide; contact when |x - k| < ~16. So I need camera such that Koopa is on screen but Mario is >20px left of it. Koopa screen x = k - camera; Mario screen x ≈ 120-ish (Mario is kept at ~1/2 screen). Actually Mario's screen x when running is around 120 (camera follows at Mario-120). So Koopa at screen 160+ means 40px ahead. Good. If Koopa at screen 190-230, safe.\n\nSo I want camera ≈ k - 200 ≈ 1450, i.e. Mario ≈ 1570. That's roughly where we were at x=1552! And the Koopa wasn't visible. Confusing.\n\nUnless the Koopa spawns only when the camera gets to k - 272? Hmm.\n\nLet me just do the incremental approach and observe.\n\nActually, wait. Maybe at x=1552, the Koopa WAS there but I misread the screenshot. Let me re-look at that screenshot: it showed two brown blobs near Mario at screen x~118 and ~150 on the hill, with \"100 100\". Those were star-killed Goombas. And the right side (screen x 200-250) had a bush/hill. No green Koopa.\n\nHmm. And there was a white shape at top right (~250,75) — maybe a cloud edge.\n\nOK. Alternatively, maybe the Koopa spawns to the RIGHT of Mario only after camera passes a threshold, and at x=1552 the camera 1426 hadn't reached it. The spawn condition in SMB: the enemy is spawned when the camera's x is >= enemy x - 0x110? Hmm.\n\nAnyway, empirical: from current x=1697 the Koopa is behind at ~1666. So it spawned somewhere between camera 1436 and 1571.\n\nNew plan: load slot 2 (x=1300, camera 1174), then run 130 frames → x~1630? That might touch it. Hmm.\n\nBetter: load slot 2, run incrementally with screenshots. Let's do 90 frames → x~1520. Check. Then 20 more.\n\nActually, I realize I can pause at will after each play_input and check. Let me do it.\n\nAlternatively, use a watch on the enemy arrays to detect the Koopa's slot. Read $0016-$001B (types) — currently [0,6,0,6,0,0] at offset 22? Wait I read address 22 length 12 = $0016-$0021. Values: [0,6,0,6,0,0,0,6,0,0,0,0]. Hmm, actually I read 12 bytes from 22: that's $0016..$0021. The first 6 = $0016-$001B: [0,6,0,6,0,0]. So enemy types: slot0=0, slot1=6, slot2=0, slot3=6, slot4=0, slot5=0. Hmm, types 0 and 6. Wait, but the mapping said enemy arrays are 6 slots. Hmm, slot0 type 0 = Goomba, slot3 type 6 = Koopa? But we saw 4 active flags [1,1,1,0,0,1]. Hmm.\n\nWait, the second read was address 110 length 12 = $006E-$0079: [7,6,7,7,7,6,0,0,6,0,0,0]. First 6 = $006E-$0073: [7,6,7,7,7,6]. Hmm pages 7,6,7,7,7,6. And $0087-$008C would be next; I didn't read. Hmm.\n\nActually, hold on: types [0,6,0,6,0,0]. Goomba type might be 0, Koopa type 6? But wait, \"0\" appears also. Hmm. Actually maybe type 0 = Goomba and type 6 = Koopa Troopa. Hmm, but SMB enemy types: Goomba=0? Koopa=6? Not standard. Actually in this \"World\" version maybe.\n\nHmm, actually wait: the first read address 15 length 12 = $000F-$001A: [1,1,1,0,0,1,0,6,0,6,0,0]. So $000F-$0014 (active) = [1,1,1,0,0,1]; $0015-$001A = [0,6,0,6,0,0]. Hmm, so $0016 = 6 and $0018=6? Wait: index0=addr15=$000F. So:\n$000F=1\n$0010=1\n$0011=1\n$0012=0\n$0013=0\n$0014=1\n$0015=0\n$0016=6\n$0017=0\n$0018=6\n$0019=0\n$001A=0\n\nSo types (if array starts at $0016): slots: $0016=6, $0017=0, $0018=6, $0019=0, $001A=0, $001B=? (not read). Hmm, so stride 1 with 6 slots starting $0016 gives [6,0,6,0,0,?]. But active flags [1,1,1,0,0,1] start at $000F, so slots 0,1,2,5 active. Mapping slot to type: slot0 type=$0016=6, slot1=$0017=0, slot2=$0018=6, slot3=$0019=0, slot4=$001A=0, slot5=$001B=? \n\nHmm. That doesn't align well (active slot5 type unknown).\n\nHmm, but wait: there's an off-by-one: the mapping says enemy arrays start at $0016 (with player at $0015?). Actually earlier mapping: \"$0016/$0087/$00CF\". And \"The same contiguous array starts one byte earlier at $0086 with the player at index 0, so enemy 1 = $0086+1 = $0087\". So for x: player at $0086, enemies at $0087-$008C. Similarly y: player $00CE, enemies $00CF-$00D4. For type: player at $0015?, enemies $0016-$001B. So slot0 = $0016. Active flags $000F-$0014 (slots 0-5).\n\nGiven active = [1,1,1,0,0,1], slots 0,1,2,5 active. Types: slot0=$0016=6, slot1=$0017=0, slot2=$0018=6, slot5=$001B=?. \n\nHmm, but from the screenshot at x=1697 we saw maybe 1 green Koopa (killed) + leftover. Hmm.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me take a much more direct approach: load slot 2, then advance in ~20-frame steps, and at each step list_sprites / screenshot until a Koopa (tiles likely $B8-$BB or similar green shell) appears. Actually the Koopa's sprite tiles: In SMB, Koopa Troopa tiles are $B8-$BF? Hmm. The Goomba is $70-$73. The Koopa (green) is likely tiles like $B8,$B9,$BA,$BB or $86-$8D etc. Hmm.\n\nActually, the dead shell we saw — let me list_sprites NOW to see the Koopa's tiles! The green thing at screen (95,197) is the dying/flipped Koopa. list_sprites will show its tiles. That gives me the Koopa's tile IDs and palette — very useful. Then I can identify it in future frames.\n\nLet me list_sprites now."}
