lock is at ground level in a brick row...\n\nHmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider the whole 1-1 layout. Maybe this ROM's 1-1 is the standard one and the ? blocks are:\n- x=336: ? (coin), part of row\n- x=368: ? (coin), part of row\n- x=400 (higher, y=88): ? — this is likely the mushroom block? Hmm, no, higher blocks are usually coins.\n\nActually in real SMB 1-1, there's a ? block high up (y=4 tiles) at around x=400? No...\n\nHmm. Let me just take a broader look. Actually, let me step back and use a smarter method to find powerup blocks: read the game's block content data.\n\nIn SMB, when the level is loaded, each object is decoded and the block contents are stored in a buffer. The routine at CPU $9508 (area object decoder) and $9595 (per-type block decoder) sets block types. The block type buffer is at $0500? Hmm.\n\nActually, the mapping says: \"the renderer at CPU $88E7-$8906 reads LDA $06A1,X (block type)\". So $06A1 is a block-type array (metatile buffer). Hmm, but that's the graphics type.\n\nFor interaction, SMB uses $0500 (block type) I think. Actually the standard SMB RAM map: \n- $0500-$05FF: \"Block_Buffer\"? \n- The block buffer for Mario's collision is at $0500-$056F? Hmm.\n\nActually I recall: SMB's block buffer is at $0500 (block type per tile) and $0600? no.\n\nHmm, let me use the mapping's lead: \"$06A1,X (block type)\" is read at CPU $88E7 during rendering. And in the same routine they compute the tile pointer. So $06A1 might be the metatile/block type buffer. When Mario hits a block, the routine reads the block type from the same buffer to decide the content.\n\nActually, I recall in SMB, the \"block buffer\" is at $0500, and the \"metatile buffer\" at $06A1 for rendering. Hmm.\n\nLet me try differential instead: \n- Hit a coin block (we know 336 and 368 are coins).\n- Hit the mushroom block elsewhere.\nBut I don't know where the mushroom is.\n\nBetter: use the level layout data at prgRom 0x2690. The ? block objects are type $C0 (per mapping: \"t