tually, hold on — maybe the block buffer value is $C0 for \"? block\" and the CONTENT is determined by the block's position via the level object type. And when Mario hits it, the game looks up the object type from... hmm.\n\nOK. Let me try a totally different, guaranteed approach: find a powerup block by looking at the SMB 1-1 level layout properly using the ROM's object type table.\n\nLet me find the object type table in the ROM. The decoder at $95e2 handles types < $0C: LDA #$16 / STA $07; then LDA (E7),Y / AND #$0F. Hmm, $07=$16. Hmm, that's probably the \"block type\" written to the buffer! So for object types 0-11, the block type = $16?? Hmm, that doesn't match $C0.\n\nHmm wait, $07 might be an index into the block graphics table.\n\nHmm.\n\nOK, let me just brute force the high ? block by precise positioning. Now I know Mario can stand on the row. Let me place Mario ON the row via y write, then jump.\n\nThe row top is y=144. Small Mario standing on it: sprite top $00CE = 144-16 = 128. Let me write x=356 ($006D=1,$0086=100), y=128, and step. Earlier that made him vanish. But maybe the issue was the camera/x. Let me try again and diagnose with list_sprites.\n\nActually — hmm, what if the game's \"player is on the ground\" is determined by a flag and placing him at y=128 makes him fall; then when he lands on the row, the sprite should reappear. My earlier attempt: after 3+6 steps, no sprite. Maybe he fell through the row and died (fall death). Hmm, but the row is solid.\n\nHmm, actually — maybe the issue is that y=128 with x=356 places his BODY (128-144) exactly on top of the row (144-159)? No, feet at 144 = row top. Fine.\n\nHmm, unless the game's collision uses the FEET at y+16=144, and the row occupies 144-159; standing exactly at the boundary. He should be fine.\n\nHmm, maybe the game's sprite routine hides Mario when $00CE has a value that doesn't match the physics ($03B8 vs $00CE). Hmm.\n\nHmm, let me look at the earlier failure: after load slot 6 + writes + step 3, I read $00CE=128, $03AD=61, game_mode=1, lives=2. And list_sprites found no Mario. And the screenshot showed no Mario. But $03AD=61 (his screen x). So the game computed his screen x. But no OAM.\n\nHmm! Maybe the player sprite routine wasn't called because the game thinks he's not \"active\"? OR maybe the OAM entries for Mario were there but with tile $FC (blank) — list_sprites filters? No, it showed the HUD coin and would show blank entries.\n\nHmm, actually list_sprites might filter out off-screen sprites. If Mario's OAM y was such that he's off-screen... $00CE=128 → OAM y=128, on-screen. Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait — maybe the issue: the game's player sprite routine uses the player's \"sprite form\" index which depends on the power state, and my write glitched it. Hmm.\n\nOK, forget it. Let me try a different tactic for the high block: have Mario jump from the row's TOP. To get on the row, I'll use the run-jump from the left (which worked — he got on the row: the y watch showed him at y=112 at frame 40, which is standing on the row (feet 128? hmm, y=112 → feet 128, but row top is 144; so y should be 128). Hmm, y=112 means feet at 128, which is ABOVE the row top (144). So he was standing on the HIGH BLOCK? No...\n\nHmm, y=112: if Mario's sprite is 16 tall, feet at 128. The row top is at 144. So feet at 128 = 16px above the row. Hmm, that means he was standing on... the high block? The high block top is at 80. No.\n\nHmm, confusing. Actually $00CE is the top of Mario's sprite, and for small Mario the sprite is 16 tall, so feet = $00CE+16. At $00CE=112, feet=128. The row top = 144. So he's floating 16px above the row. Hmm, maybe he was standing on the ? block at the row? The row's ? blocks top at 144 too.\n\nHmm. Actually, maybe the y watch at frame 40 caught him mid-landing.\n\nOK, I've spent enormous effort. Let me step back and pick the most reliable approach available: \n\n**Approach: modify the coin block's type at hit-time to make it spawn a powerup.** I'll examine the block-hit routine in the ROM to find the content table.\n\nLet me find the block-hit routine. The coin award is at CPU $BB7D (JSR $BBFE). Let me find who calls the routine containing $BB7D. Actually $BB38/$BB51 are spawn helpers, and $BB96 is the \"bump\" updater. The hit-block dispatch is probably around $BAxx.\n\nLet me disassemble CPU $BA00 length 256 to find the block type comparison. Actually, let me look for where the block type ($C0 etc.) is read. The block under Mario... Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, let me find the \"powerup spawn\" routine. It might be a JSR to $BC60 (the routine I found that reads $39). Actually $BC60's routine sets $23=1, $14=1, etc. — it looks like the \"spawn powerup\" routine! It's called somewhere.\n\nLet me find the callers of $BC60. Search PRG for \"20 60 bc\" (JSR $BC60).\n\nLet me search."}
