{"type":"thinking","text":"No JSR $BC60. Hmm. So $BC60 is reached differently (maybe a JMP or it's a continuation of another routine). Let me disassemble from $BC00 to find the routine entry and see the block-type logic.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Perhaps the routine containing $BC60 starts earlier. Let me disassemble CPU $BBF0 length 128 (i.e., $BBF0-$BC6F).\n\nActually, I did disassemble $BB00 length 192 earlier which covered up to $BBBF. Let me disassemble $BBC0 length 128 to cover $BBC0-$BC3F. And $BBF0.\n\nHmm. Actually, the key question: where's the \"block content\" decision? Let me search for the coin block's type value comparison. When Mario hits a block, the routine reads the block type and decides. Let me trace it: trace_writes on $0647 during a block hit would give the writing PC! That PC is in the block-hit routine, and I can disassemble backwards to find the content logic.\n\nLet me do trace_writes on $0647 (address 1607) while hitting a block.\n\nHmm, but trace_writes refuses while paused (frames must run). So I need to set it up then let the game run and hit a block. I can use play_input... but trace_writes is a separate call that runs frames. Sequence:\n1. load slot 4 (Mario under left ? block, paused).\n2. trace_writes address 1607, frames 30 — but this runs the game 30 frames with no input, so Mario won't jump. Hmm. I need the jump to happen during the trace.\n\nHmm, trace_writes runs frames but I can't inject input. Hmm. Unless I first start a play_input... no, they're separate.\n\nAlternative: write $0647 with a sentinel value first? No, the game overwrites.\n\nHmm. Actually, maybe I can set up so the block is hit automatically: e.g., Mario is falling onto the block? Or a Goomba bumps it? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively: use trace_writes with mode write on $0647 and press A via... hmm.\n\nHmm, trace_writes only runs frames; no input. But if Mario is already mid-jump heading into the block, the hit happens within the trace! Let me set up: get Mario just below the block, mid-jump (rising), pause, then trace_writes for 20 frames. During those frames, Mario continues his jump and hits the block. \n\nTo do that: play_input [{a,6}] (jump starts, Mario rising), then immediately trace_writes. But play_input pauses at the end. Then trace_writes runs 20 frames from that paused state — but does it resume? trace_writes \"installs a callback on the range for N frames\" — it runs frames. If the game is paused, it might refuse (\"Refused while paused\"). Hmm, the description says \"Refused while paused (frames must run)\".\n\nSo I need the game running. Hmm. play_input unpauses and re-pauses. Hmm.\n\nWhat if I set $0776 (pause flag) = 0 and resume? Hmm, but the tool-level pause is Mesen pause, which trace_writes can't run through.\n\nHmm. Actually, \"play_input ... a paused game is resumed for the run and paused again after\" — so play_input can run frames from paused. And trace_writes needs frames to run... maybe it also resumes? The description says \"Refused while paused (frames must run)\".\n\nHmm. Alternatively, use play_input with a watch and no trace. Hmm.\n\nOK, forget trace_writes.\n\nLet me take the pragmatic brute-force: test the high ? block by precisely landing Mario on the row and jumping. Let me improve the run-jump.\n\nFrom the last run: Mario ended at x=345, y=48 (in the air, past the block). He jumped from the row and overshot to the left? Hmm, x=345 < block 352. So he jumped from x~330 (on the row?) and didn't reach the block. Hmm.\n\nHmm, actually the y watch: from y=168, the first jump peaked at 111 (frames 26-28) then descended to 112 (frame 36-40) — that's him landing on the row? y=112 → feet 128, row top 144?? Doesn't match.\n\nHmm, wait: maybe he landed on the LEFT ? BLOCK (336-351)! Its top is 144. Standing on it, feet 144 → y=128. But the watch showed 112 at frames 36-40. Hmm, 112 vs 128. 16px off. Hmm, maybe small Mario's $00CE is the sprite top and the sprite is 16 tall but the collision box is 16... hmm.\n\nHmm, actually, maybe $00CE=112 corresponds to standing on the high ? block (top 80)? No.\n\nHmm. Hmm, wait, maybe he was standing on the ROW but the row's top is at 128, not 144? Let me recheck: the nametable row 18 = y 144-159. So the row's top is 144. Mario standing on it: feet=144 → $00CE = 144-16 = 128. The watch showed 112. Hmm, 16 less. So maybe $00CE is the sprite's center or the feet minus something.\n\nFrom the mapping: \"$00CE: on the ground in 1-1 it reads 176\". Ground top = 176. Mario's feet on the ground = 176 (ground row 22 = y 176). So $00CE = 176 = the ground top = feet level! So $00CE is the FEET, not the top. Hmm, but the mapping says \"screen y of the top of Mario's sprite box\". Hmm, conflicting.\n\nIf $00CE = feet: on the ground 176. Then standing on the row (top 144) → $00CE = 144. The watch showed 112 at frames 36-40; that would be feet at 112, i.e., standing on something 64px above ground = the high block? No.\n\nHmm, y=112... Hmm, what if he landed on the ? block at the row (top 144) → $00CE=144. Not 112.\n\nHmm, 112 = 176-64. Hmm.\n\nHmm, whatever.\n\nLet me just try again with more careful stepping and screenshots. Actually, let me try a simpler direct method: place Mario ON the row via writes, ONE step, and screenshot immediately.\n\nLet me write x=356, y=144 (if $00CE=feet) or y=128 (if top). Let me try y=128 first (as before) and check. Hmm, earlier y=128 vanished.\n\nLet me try y=144. Then step 1, list_sprites.\n\nActually, from the mapping's write proof: \"write_memory $00CE = 120 (0x78) while paused and stepping 2 frames left Mario hanging in mid-air at y~120\". So $00CE=120 → sprite at y~120. So $00CE IS the sprite top. OK. So standing on the row → $00CE=128.\n\nEarlier with y=128, Mario vanished. Hmm. Let me retry and check more carefully (maybe he was there but the star flash hid him?).\n\nOK let me just try once more: load slot 4, write x=356, y=128, $00B5=0, step 1, list_sprites.\n\nHmm, but if he vanishes again, I'm stuck.\n\nHmm, actually, maybe he \"vanished\" because he was standing INSIDE the ? block? At x=356, the high block is at 352-367 y=80-95; the row's brick at 352-367 y=144-159. Mario at y=128 (body 128-144). That's between the high block (95) and the row (144) — a 49px gap. So he'd be in the air at y=128 with feet at 144 (on the row). Fine, not inside anything.\n\nHmm.\n\nOK, let me just try the run-jump again but with a better script, or let me try a different approach entirely: \n\n**Use the block at level 336 but as BIG Mario.** Wait — if the block at 336 is a COIN block, big Mario gets a coin. No good.\n\nHmm, hmm.\n\nOK, let me reconsider: maybe the high ? block is NOT reachable and not the mushroom. Maybe the mushroom block is elsewhere. Let me just explore the rest of the level with jumps. I'll go from x=641 and continue.\n\nActually, let me think about the real 1-1 again: The mushroom ? block... \n\nYou know what? I bet the answer is this: in real SMB 1-1, the row of bricks near the start (brick,?,brick,?...) has the LEFT ? block giving a COIN and the SECOND ? block giving the MUSHROOM? But I tested the second (368) and got a coin. Hmm.\n\nHmm, OR the mushroom is in the high ? block (352). Let me just try harder to hit it.\n\nLet me try: place Mario on the row at x=340 (on the left ? block's top? no, that's a ? block). Hmm. Actually, Mario can stand on TOP of the left ? block (336-351, top 144). Then jump: at x=340, jumping up, he'd hit nothing above (the high block is at 352-367). Hmm.\n\nLet me place Mario on the row at x=356 (on the middle brick's top, y=128) and jump.\n\nLet me retry the y write and check with list_sprites AND a screenshot 1 frame later.\n\nLet me do it once more.\n\nActually, let me try writing y=130 (a hair above) so he falls onto the row. Then step several frames and check.\n\nHmm.\n\nOK, time check: I've used ~70 tool calls. Let me be decisive. Let me try the y-write once more, carefully."}
